    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAATn/+rUA\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/assignment/backends/sql.py",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 517,
      "author": {
        "id": 7500
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-11-21T17:21:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "All of the calls to flush() could be removed. All they do is effect outstanding ORM queries in the DBMS. The only behavioural difference could be that errors due to constraints enforced by the DBMS are detected and raised earlier in Python. Failing earlier, or failing fast, helps with debugging and precludes pointless work that will be rolled back.\n\nA side effect of flush() is that the ORM can drop state; assuming that the DBMS is more efficient than sqlalchemy, keeping the transaction\u0027s state in the DBMS is probably a good thing. However, this is a pretty hand-wavy argument, so I wouldn\u0027t put much stock in it without empirical validation.\n\nAnyhow, I just removed the flush() calls that happened immediately before the commits because they were truly pointless. I could go through and remove all of the flush calls, but I think the fail-fast argument is solid enough to justify keeping the flush() calls in.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAATn/+u50\u003d",
      "revId": "46a6307429831164225b3215cdae8e2feb28f9d7",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
